The vkCreateDevice function call completes successfully, which means the device handle is valid. The Segmentation fault happens immediately afterwards, either inside vkGetDeviceQueue or at the very start of createSwapChain() due to an invalid graphicsQueue handle or a missing window surface presentation check.
Fix this in main.cpp:

   1. Look at pickPhysicalDevice(). When looping through queue families to find VK_QUEUE_GRAPHICS_BIT, you MUST also explicitly verify that this queue family supports presenting to our created window surface. Add a check using VkBool32 presentSupport = VK_FALSE; vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceSupportKHR(device, index, surface, &presentSupport);. Select the queue family index ONLY if BOTH graphics support and presentSupport == VK_TRUE are satisfied.
   2. Look closely at vkGetDeviceQueue. Ensure the arguments are exactly: vkGetDeviceQueue(device, graphicsQueueFamilyIndex, 0, &graphicsQueue);. Double-check that graphicsQueue is a clean, non-local, private class member variable.
   3. At the very beginning of createSwapChain(), before doing anything else, add a strict safety assert: if (graphicsQueue == VK_NULL_HANDLE) { throw std::runtime_error("FATAL: graphicsQueue was not retrieved properly!"); }.

